mand) || index === 0)\n479:         ? {\n480:           ...recommendation,\n481:           compiledRoute,\n482:           command,\n483:           ...(Object.hasOwn(TRACKED_COMMANDS, command) ? { policy: TRACKED_COMMANDS[command].policy } : {}),\n484:         }\n485:         : { ...recommendation, compiledRoute, ...(!command && routeNeedsMode(recommendation.skillId, compiledRoute, namedSkills) ? { needsMode: true as const } : {}) };\n486:     }),\n487:   });\n488: }\n489: \n490: // A typed command is the reader's own choice, so it outranks a routed mode: the\n491: // reader already named the command, and a compiled route only guesses which mode\n492: // they meant. The top recommendation carries it because that is the row a reader\n493: // acts on, and an unrelated top keeps the output it had.\n494: export function attachTypedCommand(\n495:   output: AdvisorRecommendOutput,\n496:   prompt: string,\n497: ): AdvisorRecommendOutput {\n498:   const top = output.recommendations[0];\n499:   if (!top) return output;\n500:   const typed = typedCommandForTop(top.skillId, prompt);\n501:   if (!typed) return output;\n502:   return AdvisorRecommendOutputSchema.parse({\n503:     ...output,\n504:     recommendations: [\n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 505]","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":true},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/handlers/advisor-recommend.ts, lines 455-504\n455:     if (recommendation.command) alreadyOffered.add(recommendation.command);\n456:   }\n457:   return AdvisorRecommendOutputSchema.parse({\n458:     ...output,\n459:     recommendations: output.recommendations.map((recommendation, index) => {\n460:       // Each lookup is a subprocess, so a caller that acts on the top pick alone\n461:       // pays for that one and no more.\n462:       if (topOnly && index > 0) return recommendation;\n463:       if (!compiledRoutingEnabledForHub(recommendation.skillId)) return recommendation;\n464:       const compiledRoute = compiledRouteForRecommendation(\n465:         recommendation.skillId,\n466:         prompt,\n467:         workspaceRoot,\n468:         surfaceHint,\n469:       );\n470:       if (!compiledRoute) return recommendation;\n471:       // The route answers which mode the hub would take; the command is what a reader\n472:       // types to run it, so it rides along only when the route names exactly one mode\n473:       // that has a command. A broader route leaves the key off rather than guessing.\n474:       const command = attachCommand(recommendation.skillId, compiledRoute, prompt);\n475:       // Every caller renders the head and at most one partner, so a command a\n476:       // lower row carries would never reach the reader. The head keeps the\n477:       // command whichever request shape the caller asked for.\n478:       return command && (!alreadyOffered.has(command) || index === 0)\n479:         ? {\n480:           ...recommendation,\n481:           compiledRoute,\n482:           command,\n483:           ...(Object.hasOwn(TRACKED_COMMANDS, command) ? { policy: TRACKED_COMMANDS[command].policy } : {}),\n484:         }\n485:         : { ...recommendation, compiledRoute, ...(!command && routeNeedsMode(recommendation.skillId, compiledRoute, namedSkills) ? { needsMode: true as const } : {}) };\n486:     }),\n487:   });\n488: }\n489: \n490: // A typed command is the reader's own choice, so it outranks a routed mode: the\n491: // reader already named the command, and a compiled route only guesses which mode\n492: // they meant. The top recommendation carries it because that is the row a reader\n493: // acts on, and an unrelated top keeps the output it had.\n494: export function attachTypedCommand(\n495:   output: AdvisorRecommendOutput,\n496:   prompt: string,\n497: ): AdvisorRecommendOutput {\n498:   const top = output.recommendations[0];\n499:   if (!top) return output;\n500:   const typed = typedCommandForTop(top.skillId, prompt);\n501:   if (!typed) return output;\n502:   return AdvisorRecommendOutputSchema.parse({\n503:     ...output,\n504:     recommendations: [\n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 505]"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"limit\":50,\"offset\":455,\"path\":\".skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/handlers/advisor-recommend.ts\"}"},"time":{"start":1794680616632,"end":1794680616872}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794680616898,"sessionID":"ses_e24b9ad87ffe4Yqg6PDGQGxiHx","part":{"partID":"prt_1db4cf5a2002BKSv14PqKmos6w","sessionID":"ses_e24b9ad87ffe4Yqg6PDGQGxiHx","messageID":"msg_1db4c62190016m2TaF8DS0h2n7","type":"tool","id":"call_1f992792699640a2a04e0546","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research && node --version && head -30 .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts | grep -n \"import\\|from\" ; sed -n '100,130p' .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/README.md"},"output":"v26.13.0\n7:// it. This module owns that translation and nothing else, kept free of imports\n11:// derived from a hub's own command files, so a hub that adds or renames a mode\n12:// regenerates its row instead of drifting from a copy someone forgot to update.\n\nThe Skill Advisor bridge plugin injects routing advice at prompt time.\n\n- Constant advisor policy is delivered in full on the first proven message and after registered lifecycle boundaries.\n- Route-only repeats require confirmed primitive identity, valid transcript evidence, matching versioned state, stable generation/epoch clocks and an atomically advanced high-water mark.\n- When there is no brief, the plugin and the hooks emit a fallback that opens with one status line (`Advisor: outage (...)`, `Advisor: no skill matched.` or `Advisor: prompt skipped.`).\n- For setup-shaped prompts, no-match and outage fallbacks also name `/doctor:setup`.\n- A repeat in a confirmed session keeps that line and drops the directives.\n- With the opt-in `deduplicateTransforms` on, the plugin decides same-message transform dedup on the full block before lifecycle reduction, so a repeated transform for the same message is suppressed.\n- Full-delivery receipts commit after stdout handoff. A boundary failure poisons older receipts, and unsafe or unavailable durable state stays full.\n- `SPECKIT_DIRECTIVE_LIFECYCLE_DEDUP=0` restores always-full delivery.\n\nThe separate `/goal` plugin:\n\n- Persists a session objective, injects a bounded active-goal block and exposes `opencode_goal` and `opencode_goal_status`.\n- Its command router delegates all state reads and writes to `opencode_goal` and `opencode_goal_status`.\n- Active continuation remains opt-in through `OPENCODE_GOAL_AUTONOMY`.\n- Live OpenCode-run tool invocation is verified: an `opencode serve` run lists `opencode_goal` and `opencode_goal_status` in the session tool set. A live model turn persists per-session state.\n\n### Doctor Command Routing\n\nWhen no skill answers a setup or health prompt, the fallback names the runnable `/doctor:setup` line. When a skill and doctor bridge both pass without a typed `/doctor:` command, the skill stays first and the brief adds `Also: /doctor:<command> <target> (<policy>)` as its second line, where the policy is run, start or ask, or `parent-named only` in a child session for a command the child must not start on its own, and leaves the line out when the head already names that command.\n\n---\n\n## 3. HOW IT WORKS\n\n### The Fusion and the Options\n\nThe advisor scores every prompt against the five lanes from Section 1, each producing its own evidence, then fuses them into one calibrated ranking. Public responses include per-lane contribution labels so hook telemetry stays safe to log without revealing the user's prompt text.\n\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"v26.13.0\n7:// it. This module owns that translation and nothing else, kept free of imports\n11:// derived from a hub's own command files, so a hub that adds or renames a mode\n12:// regenerates its row instead of drifting from a copy someone forgot to update.\n\nThe Skill Advisor bridge plugin injects routing advice at prompt time.\n\n- Constant advisor policy is delivered in full on the first proven message and after registered lifecycle boundaries.\n- Route-only repeats require confirmed primitive identity, valid transcript evidence, matching versioned state, stable generation/epoch clocks and an atomically advanced high-water mark.\n- When there is no brief, the plugin and the hooks emit a fallback that opens with one status line (`Advisor: outage (...)`, `Advisor: no skill matched.` or `Advisor: prompt skipped.`).\n- For setup-shaped prompts, no-match and outage fallbacks also name `/doctor:setup`.\n- A repeat in a confirmed session keeps that line and drops the directives.\n- With the opt-in `deduplicateTransforms` on, the plugin decides same-message transform dedup on the full block before lifecycle reduction, so a repeated transform for the same message is suppressed.\n- Full-delivery receipts commit after stdout handoff. A boundary failure poisons older receipts, and unsafe or unavailable durable state stays full.\n- `SPECKIT_DIRECTIVE_LIFECYCLE_DEDUP=0` restores always-full delivery.\n\nThe separate `/goal` plugin:\n\n- Persists a session objective, injects a bounded active-goal block and exposes `opencode_goal` and `opencode_goal_status`.\n- Its command router delegates all state reads and writes to `opencode_goal` and `opencode_goal_status`.\n- Active continuation remains opt-in through `OPENCODE_GOAL_AUTONOMY`.\n- Live OpenCode-run tool invocation is verified: an `opencode serve` run lists `opencode_goal` and `opencode_goal_status` in the session tool set. A live model turn persists per-session state.\n\n### Doctor Command Routing\n\nWhen no skill answers a setup or health prompt, the fallback names the runnable `/doctor:setup` line. When a skill and doctor bridge both pass without a typed `/doctor:` command, the skill stays first and the brief adds `Also: /doctor:<command> <target> (<policy>)` as its second line, where the policy is run, start or ask, or `parent-named only` in a child session for a command the child must not start on its own, and leaves the line out when the head already names that command.\n\n---\n\n## 3. HOW IT WORKS\n\n### The Fusion and the Options\n\nThe advisor scores every prompt against the five lanes from Section 1, each producing its own evidence, then fuses them into one calibrated ranking. Public responses include per-lane contribution labels so hook telemetry stays safe to log without revealing the user's prompt text.\n\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\":\"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research && node --version && head -30 .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/lib/command-attach.ts | grep -n \\\"import\\\\|from\\\" ; sed -n '100,130p' .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/README.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794680616354,"end":1794680616898}}}}
